To understand how Harrison Chase developed LangChain from scratch, imagine you are building your own framework instead of using one. That's essentially what he did.

Step 1: Identify a Common Problem (2022)

In 2022, developers were experimenting with GPT-3. Everyone kept writing the same types of code:

import openai

response = openai.ChatCompletion.create(
    model="gpt-3",
    messages=[{"role":"user","content":"Hello"}]
)

Then they also needed code to:

Read PDFs
Read CSV files
Connect to databases
Store chat history
Search documents
Call APIs

Every project repeated these tasks. Harrison noticed this pattern at AI meetups and in his own work.

Step 2: Build the Smallest Possible Library

Instead of building a huge framework, he started with a very small Python package—around 800 lines of code—on his personal GitHub account.

The first idea was:

Developer
      │
      ▼
 LangChain
      │
      ▼
    GPT API

Instead of writing GPT code repeatedly, developers could write:

llm = OpenAI()
answer = llm.invoke("Hello")
Step 3: Create a Standard Interface

Different AI companies had different APIs.

Example:

OpenAI
openai.ChatCompletion.create(...)
Hugging Face
model.generate(...)
Cohere
co.generate(...)

Each API looked different.

Harrison's solution was:

llm.invoke(prompt)

No matter which model you used, you interacted with it through the same interface.

          llm.invoke()
          /    |    \
         /     |     \
 OpenAI  Groq Gemini Claude

This "model-neutral" approach became one of LangChain's main design goals.

Step 4: Build Prompt Templates

Developers often repeated prompts like:

Translate this:
{text}

Instead of rewriting them, he created reusable templates.

Without LangChain:

prompt = f"Translate {text}"

With LangChain:

PromptTemplate(
    template="Translate {text}"
)

Now the same prompt could be reused with different inputs.

Step 5: Solve Conversation Memory

GPT forgets previous conversations unless you send the earlier messages again.

Example:

User: My name is Sugumar.

User: What's my name?

Without memory:

"I don't know."

So he added memory components.

Conversation
       │
       ▼
Memory
       │
       ▼
LLM

The framework automatically included previous messages in each request.

Step 6: Build Chains

Many applications required multiple steps.

Example:

Question
     │
     ▼
Search PDF
     │
     ▼
Summarize
     │
     ▼
Translate
     │
     ▼
Final Answer

Without LangChain, you'd manually call one function after another.

He created the concept of a Chain, where outputs from one step became inputs to the next.

Step 1
   │
Step 2
   │
Step 3
Step 7: Connect External Tools

LLMs cannot directly:

Search Google
Query databases
Use calculators
Access weather APIs

He created a common "Tool" interface.

          Agent
            │
   ┌────────┼────────┐
   │        │        │
Weather   Search   Calculator

The LLM could call these tools when needed.

